            <ArticleTitle>[The MEU classification system for distal radius fractures: Prognostic and therapeutic value of an independent assessment of various fracture parameters].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S28-S33</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2468-1229(16)30120-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.hansur.2016.03.008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Distal radius fractures (DRF) are often complex injuries that can impact the radial metaphysis (M), the radial epiphysis (E) and the distal ulna (U). Each of these parameters can influence the outcome. In a given injury, these three DRF components are involved to a varying degree and are variably associated. The MEU classification independently analyzes the three main bone components of the fracture; thus, all possible combinations and each specific injury can be described. It accurately depicts the type and severity of the DRF. Our results show that this classification is useful for both prognosis and treatment. The criteria are simple and easy to determine, making the system reliable and reproducible. The classification system uses rigorous and validated criteria to define fracture instability: any fracture for which M&gt;2 and/or E&gt;2 (severe fracture) is more likely to be associated with secondary displacement, DRUJ pain, and patient dissatisfaction. Furthermore, a metaphyseal fracture entering the DRUJ (M') and the presence of a displaced ulnar fracture (U&gt;1) affect the functional outcome, thus these two features must also be included in the classification system.</AbstractText>
